    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **54 tests, 0 failures, 0 errors, BUILD SUCCESS**.\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the direct level-computation rule with a points system that then maps to a level.\n\n**Core logic** \u2014 `AssignMembershipLevel.java`:\n- Scores `membershipPoints` starting at 0: **+2** email present, **+1** `namesakeCount == 0`, **+2** household of 3+ (`householdSize >= 3`), **+3** tenure over 365 days.\n- Maps points to `membershipLevel`: 1 (0\u20131), 2 (2\u20133), 3 (4\u20135), 4 (6+).\n- Sets **both** fields on the owner. (Level 4 still requires the tenure factor, so a fresh owner \u2014 zero tenure \u2014 never exceeds level 3, matching Cp39.)\n\n**Supporting changes so both fields round-trip:**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 new `membershipPoints` field (`membership_points` column) with getter/setter.\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added the `membershipPoints` response property and updated `membershipLevel` (now 1\u20134, points-derived). The `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec and mapped by name via MapStruct, so no mapper change was needed.\n- `db/{h2,hsqldb,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `membership_points` column.\n\nThe household-size and namesake steps already run before this step in `owners.POST.yml`, so both inputs are populated when points are scored.",
